\title{Product Topology}
\taxon{Definition}
\p{
    Let #{\{X_i\}_{i\in I}} be a family of topological spaces. The
    \strong{product topology} on the Cartesian product
    #{\prod_{i\in I}X_i} has as a basis the sets
    ##{
        \prod_{i\in I}U_i,
    }
    where each #{U_i} is open in #{X_i} and #{U_i=X_i} for all but finitely
    many indices #{i}. Equivalently, it is the [coarsest topology](def-005X)
    that makes
    every coordinate projection
    ##{
        \pi_i:\prod_{j\in I}X_j\longrightarrow X_i
    }
    continuous.
}
\p{
    For a finite product #{X_1\times\cdots\times X_n}, the basic open sets are
    simply products #{U_1\times\cdots\times U_n} of open sets. For an infinite
    product, the finite-coordinate restriction distinguishes the product
    topology from the generally [finer](def-005X) box topology.
}
